finding: Precedence can still misname the Firefox cause. src/engine/runtime/gpu.ts:762-766 ends collection on a zero-delay timer as soon as onSubmittedWorkDone settles, although that fence does not fence delivery of uncaptured errors (and the timer and WebGPU events use different task sources). Independently reproduced against HEAD without a GPU: use real Dawn GPUUncapturedErrorEvent/GPUError objects, a queue fence returning Promise.resolve(), deliver validation now, out-of-memory after 2 ms, and unknown loss after 143 ms, matching the recorded Firefox offsets. The sole console.error/verdict is GpuValidationError; the actual out-of-memory is only console.warn. The 250 ms ceiling does not help because finish already ran. The Firefox test conceals this by holding its artificial fence unresolved until after injecting both out-of-memory and loss; it proves ranking only when all candidates precede a manually released fence, not the binding Firefox-order claim. Exercise asynchronous delivery with an already-settled fence, and use a collection policy that retains that cause rather than treating one timer task as error-delivery completion.
